WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:09.870
Pete: So, what are the basic things that most
mobile OS's do in terms of helping enforce

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:10.870
security?

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:16.090
Like, what in these features in these, perhaps
somewhat immature, OS's that you mention that

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:17.720
developers can take advantage of?

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:18.720
Mike: Sure.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:20.650
So, I'll cover two at a very high level.

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:23.760
We'll talk about application sandboxing first
of all.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.890
And, that basically means your application
is running on the device and it's in its own

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:29.640
protected sandbox.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:35.070
It can potentially write to the file system,
it can write and read to its own files, but

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:39.329
it can't necessarily touch the files of another
application.

00:00:39.329 --> 00:00:45.829
So, if your banking application is storing
some sort of potentially sensitive data on

00:00:45.829 --> 00:00:52.210
a file, the newest game that you've downloaded
can't necessarily, out of the box, reach in

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:55.670
and touch- you know, read- those bits of data.

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:58.700
The operating system won't let that happen.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:04.280
Now, when the device is jailbroken, those
controls are removed.

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:09.530
And now, a rogue or malicious application
running on the device is running at a higher

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:12.540
privilege level and can potentially do a lot
of things.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:17.380
It can attack other applications, it can abuse
other bits of functionality on the phone,

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:23.380
like turn on the microphone, for example,
steal your contacts or send out SMS messages

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:27.260
or email.

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:31.430
The other bit that I'd like to talk about,
would be security data storage.

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:35.790
So, I've already talked a little bit about
just data storage on the device- writing to

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:36.790
files.

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:41.570
You don't want to- if you're, for example,
storing credentials on the device, or you

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:45.340
have the need to store credentials on a device
so that a user doesn't need to enter them

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:48.960
every time, they authenticate to your app.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:53.290
You don't want to just write them to a flat
text file in plaintext on the device.

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:58.030
Something like that is easily recoverable,
whether or not the device is jailbroken.

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:01.700
And, we're talking about applications using
it.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:05.890
If the person loses their device, you don't
want somebody to be able to pick it up and,

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:11.760
with minimal skill or ability, be able to
pull a file off your device and now have your

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.150
banking credentials.

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:21.709
So, the platforms provide certain methods
of securely storing data on the devices.

00:02:21.709 --> 00:02:25.230
For example, on iOS, there's the keychain.

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:30.579
Data stored in the keychain isn't necessarily
encrypted unless the user has a passcode on

00:02:30.579 --> 00:02:31.579
their device.

00:02:31.579 --> 00:02:36.849
So, if they don't have a passcode on their
device, and as a developer, you're storing

00:02:36.849 --> 00:02:42.830
something in the keychain, and the device
is not jailbroken, you have the application

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:43.830
sandboxing.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:47.590
So, my app can't access your app's credentials.

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:52.560
But, if an attacker picks up the device and
has physical access to it, he can get at that

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.319
keychain data and it's not protected without
the passcode.

00:02:55.319 --> 00:02:58.889
So, there are lots of little nuances that
people need to be aware of.

00:02:58.889 --> 00:03:02.969
Pete: Do you think developers are commonly
aware that this sort of scenario exists?

00:03:02.969 --> 00:03:08.879
For instance, on the iOS platform?

00:03:08.879 --> 00:03:15.079
Mike: I think the documentation could always
be improved upon.

00:03:15.079 --> 00:03:20.749
And there's a lot of just general awareness
that needs to happen.

00:03:20.749 --> 00:03:28.349
It's also very easy to go down the encryption
rabbit hole, 'OK, we know we need to protect

00:03:28.349 --> 00:03:34.780
this data, so let's encrypt it and we'll use
a symmetric key.'

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:39.139
You come across that quite often when you're
reviewing mobile applications.

00:03:39.139 --> 00:03:44.090
You just happen to find a hardcoded encryption
code right in the application code base, used

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:47.969
to store the credentials and the file system
"encrypted".

00:03:47.969 --> 00:03:54.329
But, what the developers don't always realize
is that that key is easily recoverable, and

00:03:54.329 --> 00:04:00.930
now, in that case, OK, now the key is on a
million devices for the one million users

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:02.069
that have installed your application.

00:04:02.069 --> 00:04:09.290
So, now you've got a situation where a bad
buy can say, 'OK, well, I see how poorly this

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:14.629
app is protecting this sensitive data, I can
see, based on the number of downloads, that

00:04:14.629 --> 00:04:20.989
it's installed on bizillions of devices, now
I can target this application.

00:04:20.989 --> 00:04:28.370
And, I can write a trojan or some piece of
malware that will masquerade as a legitimate

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:34.590
mobile application and seek out this other
app on the installed user base.'

